$\because\left(x^2+\frac{1}{x^2}+2\right)^n=\left[\left(x+\frac{1}{x}\right)^2\right]^n=\left(x+\frac{1}{x}\right)^{2 n}$<br/>The number of terms in the expansion of $\left(x+\frac{1}{x}\right)^{2 n}$ is $2 n+1$ which is odd. Therefore, $(n+1)$ th term will be middle term.
$\begin{aligned}
\therefore \quad T_{n+1} & ={ }^{2 n} C_n(x)^{2 n-n}\left(\frac{1}{x}\right)^n \\
& ={ }^{2 n} C_n x^n \cdot \frac{1}{x^n} \\
& ={ }^{2 n} C_n=\frac{(2 n)!}{n!n!}=\frac{(2 n)!}{(n!)^2}
\end{aligned}$

